00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.710 align:start position:0%
 
now a new report out today says that  many people will be up to sixteen  hundred pounds worse off this year  because of rising energy costs and  rocketing inflation so can the tory  leadership candidates offer any help  well joining me now is former defense  secretary and liz trust supporter penny  mordant a very good morning to you penny  morton thanks so much for talking to us  here today  first of all when you were standing for  number 10 you said that your plans for  the economy were based on  sound money  tackling inflation to help with the cost  of living and targeted tax cuts  can you genuinely say that liz truss's  plans tick any or all of those boxes  yes so i was the candidate sort of the  halfway house between rishi and liz's  policy platforms and the reason why i  have chosen to support liz in this  contest  i could have remained  undeclared but i i think this is too  important the reason why i've gone with  her  is because i think she does understand  that our nation is at a an inflection  point and all of the opportunities that  we want to see come to us investment and  other opportunities aren't going to land  on our lap we've got to work for them  we've got to be able to compete we've  got to get more productive we've got to  get supply-side reforms to happen  and that is something that liz does  understand so that is why i've gone with  her her tax cuts are funded she has a  plan uh she has a plan to get growth  back into our economy and that is what  we need to do  well yes but going by your  prime principles that you laid out you  talked about the tax cuts for example  but tax cuts paid through borrowing have  the risk of being inflationary this is  something that the former deputy  governor of the bank of england told me  yesterday he said there's a real risk  that will prompt inflation you said  tackling inflation was one of your  priorities  on the issue of sound money liz trust  has announced tens of billions of pounds  of tax cuts paid through  headroom which may no longer be there  because of the rising rates of inflation  and when it comes to targeted tax cuts  which is another thing you said you  wanted at the moment the reversal of the  rise in national insurance benefits  people on higher salaries so much more  on those on lower salaries so i'm just  wondering do you really feel that she's  living up to the the ideals that you set  out when you were running for leadership  yes of course i've had conversations  with both rishi and liz since i was  eliminated from the contest uh tax cuts  don't have to be inflationary because  we're dealing with the twin problems of  inflation but also weak demand  in the economy her tax cutting program  is funded  it is going to matter very much the  sequence and timing of when things are  done i think she's been right to  put forward a  an emergency budget  clearly there's support for that from  other quarters in the papers today  both in terms of addressing the cost of  living issues but also to really get  growth back into the economy it's all  very well alleviating a problem and the  the squeeze that people are feeling  but we've got to get growth back into  the economy if we don't want that to  become the norm well let's talk about  the the squeeze that people are facing  liz trust has made it clear that she  wants to handle it through tax cuts  rather than handouts as she puts it but  today's poverty report is showing that  four in every five of the country's lone  parents pensioners and large families  are going to be in fuel poverty after  the rises in inflation so is this trust  genuinely saying that  she won't be expanding direct payments  to support them because she doesn't want  to go against conservative orthodoxy no  she's not she's not ruled out all future  help in fact part of her the reason for  her putting an emergency budget forward  is to really address some of these  issues but what she has i think rightly  challenged is the wisdom of taking large  sums of money out of people's pockets in  tax and then giving some of it back in  ever more complicated ways she's making  a general point about the merits of  enabling people to keep more of what  they earn and i think that is the right  principle well no she said she didn't  want to do to help people via handouts  but you're saying that she will consider  handouts she's not she's not ruled out  further help she's already put forward  uh some uh help for people this autumn  clearly the government has already done  a lot i was speaking to nadim zahaway  yesterday  about some of the things that he's  looking at so that whoever is coming in  has got some some further options to  look at there's a huge amount of of  focus on this and of course nadeem has  also announced for  liz truss so it's not she's ruling out  uh  all future help  that's a misinterpretation of what she  said  what she is looking at though is  enabling people to keep more of the  money that they earn it makes no sense  to take money off of people and then to  give it back in very very complicated  ways we need to simplify this and we  need to ensure that households are  resilient as possible and stopping  taking large sums of tax off people is  one way of doing that okay well let's  look at the the tax reversal of national  insurance for example how does that help  the vulnerable people those people that  this report said are going to be so  badly hit  come  later in the year people on benefits  pensioners those not earning enough to  be paying national insurance anyway so  look we can help people in a whole  variety of ways you mentioned pensioners  the older you are generally speaking the  higher your cost of living  liz is committed to the the triple lock  uh there are all sorts of things that we  are already doing in terms of uh  measures that will be brought in this  autumn but what liz also recognizes is  as well as  taking those immediate pressures off of  people you also have to deal with this  issue uh more long term and that means  getting growth back into our economy it  means not taxing jobs it means actually  stimulating the economy pushing through  a program of work that she's outlined on  supply side to really enable us to be  competitive and also addressing those  issues uh so that we're not one of the  most uncompetitive nations with regard  to tax  next april those are the things we also  have to do in tandem with helping people  day-to-day  and clearly it's important to look  further ahead but there's a very  immediate problem right now and you  mentioned pensioners a typical fuel bill  will take up a third of the statutory  pension  so  whatever you think about people people  struggling older people struggling on a  political level does it make sense then  to not tackle  not tackle this i mean it's politically  not a very clever move a lot of  conservatives are pensioners  so look there's there's things that liz  has already announced with regard to the  the green levees she wants to have a  moratorium on that uh she's looking at  other ways that uh she can uh assist  people in that but we also have to  address the underlying problems in the  economy and  just waiting for for that to to resolve  itself is not an option we actually have  to start working to ensure that we can  compete that we can  create high-wage jobs that we can do all  of those things that is actually going  to put us in a much better and much more  resilient position the the economy is  very volatile at the moment it's quite  right that we have a reset moment when a  new prime minister comes in liz has said  that she will do that and i'm interested  in supporting a candidate that  recognizes the situation we're in at the  moment but also is still ambitious about  delivering that brexit dividend liz can  do both and i've seen her in action we  came into parliament at the same time in  2010 she's someone that is pragmatic  she's evidence-led but she's absolutely  driven and determined to deliver for  this country and that's why she's got my  backing  and we've heard from the former prime  minister gordon brown today saying that  that such is the crisis that  not only liz trust rishi sunak but the  prime minister boris johnson should all  come together right now  and come up with an emergency budget he  can't wait until  a leadership  winner is is crowned if you like would  liz trust do that  so look i understand that the focus is  on the the leadership contest but there  is still a huge amount of work going on  in government as i say i spoke to the  chancellor just yesterday about some  things that he is doing  this this work continues and of course  parliament isn't sitting at the moment  but when that person comes in whether it  is rishi or liz there will be  some more options for them to to look at  they'll be able to look at the the  fiscal position uh which we're currently  looking at a red book that's very out of  date and they'll be able to take  decisions quickly on that liz has said  that she will do that she is going to  have an emergency budget that will both  address the cost of living issues and  the things we need to get our public  services really recovering from the the  pandemic but also she has her eyes on  the prize of getting real growth back  into our economy  a quick reflection on your time in the  leadership contest it got pretty toxic  at points didn't it um there were quite  a few people that seemed to be  on a concerted effort to  stop you progressing i know lord frost  an ally of liz trust was pretty scathing  to you over the brexit talks i mean he  was quite brutal he talked about you not  being a master of the dieter dale not  fully accountable not always visible how  did that feel at the time well look i  have a  different reflection on my events in in  government working with lord frost and  others but look this isn't about me it's  not about my feelings  it's about making sure that in a few  weeks time we can have a team that can  come together and deliver for this  country we have got to get people  getting access to the healthcare that  they need huge challenges obviously to  overcome uh to do that we've got to  ensure that we can give the reassurance  that people need now with regard to  their household budgets and we've got to  also enable business to really thrive to  make the investments that we want them  to make those are the things that we  need to be focused on it doesn't matter  about my ego my feelings i'm going to  work with the new prime minister to  deliver on those things and i hope that  is going to be liz truss okay well penny  morton we appreciate your time this  morning thanks for coming on and talking  to us thank you

